Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT vs JSON — Hand-written documents vs serialization
	Metadata:
		Author: Claude Fable 5
		Revision: Joan Costa Mombiela
		Last modif: 2026-09-01
		Description: STXT and JSON compared: authoring by hand, comments, multi-line text and validation — and how every STXT document maps to a canonical JSON tree.

	Header: @STXT@ vs JSON

	Assert >>
		JSON is a serialization format for machines; @STXT@ is a markup language for people.

	Content >>
		JSON does its job with a minimal grammar, one data model and parsers everywhere;
		it is the lingua franca of APIs. The comparison only makes sense at
		the point where the two overlap — a file that a person has to write and maintain by
		hand — because that is the job JSON never promised to do.

	Subheader: The same content, twice

	Content >>
		An article record in JSON:

	Json >>
		{
			"article": {
				"title": "Modern Software Architecture",
				"published": "2026-09-01",
				"tags": ["software", "architecture"],
				"summary": "A survey of monoliths and microservices.\nSecond line, with \"quotes\"."
			}
		}

	Content >>
		And in @STXT@:

	Code >>
		Article: Modern Software Architecture
			Published: 2026-09-01
			Tag: software
			Tag: architecture
			Summary >>
				A survey of monoliths and microservices.
				Second line, with "quotes".

	Subheader: What separates them when writing by hand

	Content >>
		Each difference below is a deliberate JSON decision that serves serialization, and
		gets in the way of authoring:

		* **Everything is quoted and delimited.** Keys and strings between quotes, objects
		  between braces, elements between commas — and the comma after the last element
		  is a syntax error. In @STXT@, structure is indentation and a name ends at `:`.
		* **No comments.** The exclusion is intentional in JSON; in a hand-maintained file
		  it is the first thing missed. @STXT@ has `#` comments, and tools preserve them
		  when formatting.
		* **A text is one line.** Line breaks are written `\n`, quotes are escaped, and a
		  paragraph becomes a single escaped string. In @STXT@ a `>>` block holds the
		  text literally, as long as it needs to be.
		* **No schema in the language.** JSON Schema exists, but it is a separate
		  specification, written in JSON itself, with its own vocabulary of composition
		  and references. The @STXT@ [template](stxt-template-ref) layer is part of the
		  language and reads like the document it describes.

	Subheader: From @STXT@ to JSON

	Content >>
		@STXT@ does not compete with JSON as an interchange format: it adopts it. @STXT@
		chose JSON as the representation of its logical tree, because representing a data
		structure for programs to consume is exactly the job JSON is designed for.
		[STXT-TREE-SPEC](stxt-tree-ref) defines that canonical representation for every
		valid document — an array of root nodes with `name`, `canonicalName`, `namespace`,
		and `value` plus `children`, or `lines` — identical in every implementation. The
		command `stxt describe file.stxt` prints it, and the three libraries expose it
		from their API, so anything downstream that expects JSON keeps working. The source
		stays readable; the serialization stays mechanical.

	Subheader: When to use which

	Content >>
		Use JSON for what machines exchange: API payloads, serialized state, generated
		artifacts nobody edits. Its ubiquity is the feature, and @STXT@ does not try to
		replace it there.

		Write @STXT@ when a person is on either end of the file: documents,
		[configuration](use-cases-config-files), [corporate records](use-cases-corporate-docs),
		[content that feeds a pipeline](use-cases-cms) — and let the tools emit the
		canonical tree where JSON is expected. The [AI and LLMs](ai-llms) use case shows
		that route end to end: readable generated documents, validated, consumed as JSON.

		To see the language, start with the [tutorial](tutorial) or open the examples in
		the [playground](https://play.stxt.dev); the one-paragraph version of this page is
		in the [FAQ](faq#why-not-json).
